WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.820
I try to make it seem like I'm just getting this out,

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:05.740
but I know all the moves, I know every little piece of it.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:11.000
One... Two...Three...

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.980
It's just here, just go here.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:19.040
This is what a Louis C. K. joke looks like.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.740
It's a short one, only 207 words.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:25.420
But they're 207 words that are chosen very purposefully,

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:28.480
practiced until every one counts for something.

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:31.560
Today I want to go through this joke beat by beat,

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:35.680
and explore how Louis C. K. constructs and performs his comedy.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:36.840
My girls are six and nine now.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.500
They're actually a really exciting age because they're learning to do some cool stuff.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.880
Every joke has to have a premise,

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:50.000
an idea that the inevitable punch line will snap comedically into place.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.760
The premise should be really clear,

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:56.100
whether it's implied as in some one-liners or stated outright.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.000
As Chris Rock once said,

00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.560
A lot of comedians have great jokes,

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:01.700
and they don't, like, why isn't this working?

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:05.800
It's not working because the audience does not understand the premise.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:09.100
In this case the premise has two parts, accomplished in two sentences.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:12.500
First, Louis is playing monopoly with his daughters,

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:13.560
and second,

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.200
The second part of this premise is beautifully structured.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:32.780
because besides very lucidly delivering the premise

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:37.380
that this joke is going to be how a six-year-old girl learns what it means to lose in Monopoly

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:40.500
Louis actually get some laughs out of the sentence

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:43.560
by the addition of a couple words and a strategic rhythm.

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:47.300
I mean the simplest way to end the sentence is to say that

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:51.340
she's not emotionally developed enough to handle losing at Monopoly,

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:53.440
but instead, he says, to handle her

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:01.700
In this way Louie adds two little comedic surprises in the premise

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:06.369
and make sure to include a pause so that each can be appreciated in their own beat.

00:02:06.369 --> 00:02:07.700
To handle her

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:20.440
Comedians Like Louis C.K. are experienced enough to master

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:21.980
not only their own delivery,

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:26.680
but where the laughs will occur in their jokes, and how to deal with them.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:30.020
Telling a joke is like riding a wave of feeling,

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:32.040
and if you watch Louis C.K.'s comedy, you'll see that

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:35.740
during laughs what he does is emphasize his last line.

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:38.160
It doesn't give the audience new information.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:41.080
He augments the information just given,

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:44.580
something that he actually learned how to do brilliantly from Jerry Seinfeld.

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:48.800
I'm opening for Jerry in front of like twelve hundred or twenty five hundred people,

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:50.500
and people are applauding bits.

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:53.040
And I had never encountered that, I didn't know how to handle it.

00:02:53.049 --> 00:02:56.979
I asked Jerry what to do, and he said, " you stay in the bit. "

00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:01.080
You stay in the bit they're reacting to.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:02.760
If you're angry and that's why they're applauding,

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:04.000
you stay in that energy.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:07.360
Because the Monopoly loss is dark.

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:09.880
It's heavy.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:11.840
It's not like

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:26.660
The choice of Candyland here, though Louie acts as if it was pulled out of thin air,

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:28.860
is the perfect counterpoint to Monopoly.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:33.340
It's visual. The game pops straight into your mind is an object of colourful innocence.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:37.620
The dramatization and the funny voices also performed like it was spontaneously made up.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:42.540
But the words and the images he uses, like fudgy thing and gummy twirly-o's,

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:46.060
are carefully chosen to enhance that visual sense.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:48.440
Louis is never not in control,

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:49.920
and that's important, because

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:52.540
since the premise has been so strongly set up

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:55.500
and the counterpoint drawn out so vividly,

00:03:55.500 --> 00:04:01.840
the laughs that Louis gets here are actually laughs in anticipation of a punchline not yet said.

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:06.800
The audience's already imagining how dark the Monopoly loss is going to be.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:10.980
Just watch how he gradually increases these anticipatory laughs,

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:15.180
like the audience is a fiddle, to the final three-word punchline.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:33.540
Gotta give it all to me.

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:35.900
Give it to me. That's right.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:39.620
One thing Louis doesn't get recognized for enough, in my opinion,

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:42.040
is his ability to dramatize in his jokes.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:44.900
When he acts out this conversation with his daughter,

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:48.560
the light touchy gives, especially with that hand movement right on the punch line,

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:52.060
really locks the audience into the space where he wants them to be.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:56.760
The audience needs to see this situation from a six-year-old perspective for it to work.

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:01.320
And if he succeeded, the tags or the two additional punchlines that follow

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:04.820
will be that much more devastating and hilarious.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:18.280
You're going down hard. It's really bad.

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:23.100
Casting the audience in the perspective of a six-year-old is important for another reason

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:27.200
because I think on a deeper level Louis is actually trying to make a comment here

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:31.140
on the actual ruthlessness of the economy we live in

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:34.240
where property really is taken away from people

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:38.000
sometimes by monopolistic interests with debt to spare.

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:42.240
That's the thing his daughter is not emotionally developed enough to comprehend.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:44.860
And when we see it through her eyes,

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:48.480
the reality seems even darker than it usually does.

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:51.120
Especially in the final tag,

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:09.940
You know more than anything else,

00:06:09.940 --> 00:06:15.700
I think I'm obsessed with articulation, with the magic of putting things just the right way.

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:20.340
There are 207 words in this joke, and not a single one is wasted

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:24.960
They're used either in meaning or in rhythm to contribute to the overall effect,

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:28.180
an effect that lets us see the world from a different angle,

00:06:28.180 --> 00:06:30.420
And more importantly, makes us laugh.

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:36.820
Like great poetry, great stand-up comedy is language distilled to its most potent form.

00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:41.620
And these days, few people distill it better than Louis C.K.

